        "math_academy_id": "q-4630",
        "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-x-1}.$",
        "worked_solution": "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-x-1} & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+3)}{(2x+1)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+3)}{(2x+1)\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n2x+1=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$",
